  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "What is reused",
      "items": [
        "One work list and one loop for three reasons: firm activation, a new publish, the background sweep",
        "One way back: load → EngagementLoaded → Reconciler → index. It is the same path a normal user load already uses",
        "The worker never writes the index",
        "A user who opens an engagement indexes it for free: that item leaves the work list without costing the worker a minute",
        "When every engagement of a firm is indexed, the Reconciler switches the feature on for that firm (firm_activation)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "amber",
      "title": "How the one-minute load is handled",
      "items": [
        "The minute cannot be removed: it is a hard constraint",
        "It is kept off the user-facing path: 'pending' is computed from the index, so a publish shows up in seconds without loading anything",
        "It is paid once per engagement: after indexing, later publishes do not load it again",
        "It is paid in parallel, within limits: 800,000 minutes at ~19 concurrent loads is 30 days",
        "The limit belongs to the team that owns the pods and can be changed at runtime"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "violet",
      "title": "What the worker must get right (Part 2)",
      "items": [
        "Concurrency: a global cap on loads in flight, never above what the other team allows",
        "Fairness: a cap per firm, so the 40,000-engagement firm cannot starve the 40-engagement ones",
        "Failure: timeout per load, retry with backoff, park the item after N attempts, resume from the saga log after a crash",
        "Idempotency: an item already indexed or already in flight is skipped"
      ]
    }
  ]
